# Previewing the explanation

A literate-program author does not edit code with comments around it. The explanation
is the working surface. Headings, links, prose, diagrams, and named contributions
need to remain readable while they are still changing, including before the document
is valid enough to generate anything.

The editor preview therefore has a different promise from generation. Generation
plans a complete, reproducible set of files and refuses partial output. Preview is a
read-only interpretation of the text currently visible in the editor. It should show
useful structure during an incomplete edit without claiming that invalid source is a
valid project.

## Preview and generation share meaning, not effects

Both paths begin with the same fragment header grammar, tag scanner, project ordering,
publication identities, and theme policy. They diverge at the host boundary.
Generation receives captured documents and templates, validates the complete output
plan, and may hand that plan to a transaction. Preview parses an in-memory overlay
and returns HTML to the editor's Markdown renderer. It neither writes generated
source nor replaces the generated book.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Visible[Visible literate text] --> Semantics[Shared parsing and semantic model]
    Saved[Captured project and templates] --> Engine[Complete generation]
    Semantics --> Preview[Live editor preview HTML]
    Engine --> Valid{Complete plan valid?}
    Valid -->|No| Diagnostics[Diagnostics and no files]
    Valid -->|Yes| Outputs[Planned source and publication files]
    Preview --> Screen[Read-only screen presentation]
```

The upper arrows answer “what can the author understand now?” The lower path answers
“what complete files can this captured project produce?” The diagram intentionally
omits filesystem transactions and print publication. The separation matters because
a preview refresh must never become an accidental generation request.

## Build the live view from visible text

The Markdown host asks its renderer for HTML synchronously. That request cannot pause
for a fresh recursive workspace scan. The editor repository supplies the latest
discovered project as a base, then overlays all open literate buffers and the exact
source handed to the current parser. The pure `parseProject` operation turns that
in-memory set into publication contributions, annotations, semantic targets, and
Markdown tokens without templates or output planning.

Capturing the exact parser source is important. Markdown normalization can replace
some invalid characters. If preview reconstructed source from normalized tokens, an
invalid document might accidentally acquire semantic links it did not have in the
editor. The original source remains the authority.

The implementation joins this behavior to VS Code through a Markdown-it extension.
The returned `extendMarkdownIt` hook installs fence rendering and a document wrapper
on the parser supplied by the built-in Markdown preview. The concept comes first:
Literate extends the host's normal Markdown path rather than maintaining a separate
preview editor.

## A named fence remains code, with semantic surroundings

Ordinary fences delegate to the renderer that was already installed. A valid named
fragment fence keeps its code presentation but gains a family label, stable anchors,
and navigation among contributions. Settings fences are hidden because their content
configures the project rather than forming part of the explanation.

Decoration is conditional on an exact semantic match. The current path, source line,
complete authored header, contribution role, language, and content must agree with
the parsed model. A stale token or malformed edit remains escaped code without
borrowing an older target. Missing families remain plain text rather than receiving
invented destinations.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Fence[Markdown fence token] --> Settings{Project settings fence?}
    Settings -->|Yes| Hide[Omit from preview]
    Settings -->|No| Diagram{Plain Mermaid fence?}
    Diagram -->|Yes| Source[Emit safe diagram source container]
    Diagram -->|No| Named{Accepted named contribution?}
    Named -->|No| Delegate[Use ordinary fence rendering]
    Named -->|Yes| Match{Exact current semantic match?}
    Match -->|No| Safe[Render escaped code without semantic links]
    Match -->|Yes| Decorate[Render code with family identity and navigation]
```

Each arrow is a narrowing decision for one fence. The diagram omits syntax-highlighter
details. What matters is the fail-closed final check: editor decoration follows
current semantics; it never upgrades merely similar text.

Named fragments whose language is Mermaid remain program code. Only a plain Mermaid
fence is a book diagram. This distinction prevents an implementation contribution
from being executed as preview markup merely because its generated language happens
to name the diagram syntax.

`delegate preview fences` preserves the host renderer and decorates only an exact
current contribution.

```ts : <<delegate preview fences>>=
const header = parseFragmentHeader(token.info);
if (!header) {
  return originalFence ? originalFence(tokens, idx, options, env, slf)
    : slf.renderToken(tokens, idx, options);
}
const previewToken = Object.assign(Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(token)), token);
previewToken.content = token.content.replace(/\\\<</g, '\<<');
previewToken.info = header.language;
const model = env.literatePublication as ReturnType<typeof parseProject>['publication'] | undefined;
const lookup = model && screenIndex(model);
const candidate = lookup?.lines.get(env.literatePath)?.get(token.map?.[0] ?? -1);
const part = candidate && candidate.content === token.content
  && candidate.kind === (header.append ? 'append' : 'definition')
  && lookup?.families.get(candidate.fragmentTarget)?.name === header.name && candidate.language === header.language
  && env.literateTokens?.[candidate.tokenIndex]?.info === token.info ? candidate : undefined;
const semantic = part && model ? renderScreenCode(part, model, 'preview', env.literateDestination) : undefined;
const fenceOptions = semantic !== undefined ? { ...options, highlight: () => semantic } : options;
const previewTokens = tokens.slice();
previewTokens[idx] = previewToken;
const rendered = header.language === 'mermaid'
  ? `<pre><code class="language-mermaid">${semantic ?? md.utils.escapeHtml(previewToken.content)}</code></pre>\n`
  : originalFence ? originalFence(previewTokens, idx, fenceOptions, env, slf)
    : `<pre><code>${semantic ?? md.utils.escapeHtml(previewToken.content)}</code></pre>\n`;
<<decorate preview fragments>>
```

## Screen rendering is shared across preview and publication

The preview does not rediscover fragment references by scanning rendered HTML. The
parsed project already records semantic ranges. A screen renderer overlays those
ranges on syntax-highlighted code, producing links only for accepted references.
The same renderer supports generated HTML, so contribution labels, source spans,
annotation identities, and fragment navigation have one model.

Presentation-specific destinations remain outside that model. Generated HTML can
use relative chapter links and stable anchors. The editor preview may need an opaque
host URL to cross documents. In both cases the logical target is the same publication
identity. Changing how a screen opens a destination does not change what the target
means.

Contribution navigation appears outside the code block so Previous, Base, and Next
controls do not alter copied code or indentation. Native anchors carry descriptive
labels and remain keyboard reachable. Source-line metadata lets the Markdown host
synchronize the current preview with its document.

## Cross-document navigation is a temporary capability

A raw preview link containing a filename, command, and line number would grant more
authority than the rendered document needs. It could also become stale as edits move
a contribution. Literate instead gives the preview a short-lived opaque capability
for one workspace object and one logical target.

The capability contains no filename or command. Before minting it, the host verifies
that the target belongs to the parsed model and that the workspace is trusted and
still present. Resolution to an external editor URL is bounded and cached briefly.
While resolution is pending or failed, the preview leaves the destination as text;
it does not fall back to a weaker line-link scheme.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Render as Preview renderer
    participant Host as Navigation capability adapter
    participant Browser as Preview link
    participant Project as Fresh project snapshot
    participant Editor

    Render->>Host: Request destination for logical target
    Host->>Host: Check trust, membership, target, and capacity
    Host-->>Render: Opaque resolved URL or no link
    Browser->>Host: Activate opaque capability
    Host->>Project: Rediscover and parse current visible sources
    Project-->>Host: Current location for the same target
    Host->>Host: Recheck capability and safe workspace path
    Host->>Editor: Open canonical document and select current location
```

The arrows show authority moving from a rendered identity back to fresh project
state. The original rendered line is never trusted as a current destination. The
diagram omits expiry renewal and request coalescing; those are lifecycle controls,
not changes to navigation meaning.

Capabilities expire, disappear with their workspace, and become invalid when the
extension session ends. Opening a target performs read-only discovery again, checks
canonical workspace membership and path components, opens the document, then
resolves the logical identity once more before selecting it. If any check changes
across an asynchronous boundary, navigation stops.

## Theme the project, not the editor chrome

The root document chooses the Literate screen theme. An open root buffer takes
precedence over its saved text, so a theme edit can be previewed before save. A local
chapter setting cannot silently redefine the project, and an invalid selection uses
the shared default while generation remains responsible for reporting the diagnostic.

The selected catalogue theme controls both the scoped document stylesheet and the
diagram palette. It is independent of the surrounding editor theme. Scoping prevents
a book stylesheet from restyling the preview host itself.

A semantic contribution change, annotation change, or effective theme change requests
a Markdown preview refresh only after the repository has installed the new snapshot.
Refresh is coalesced and not awaited inside synchronous rendering. This ordering
prevents a fresh preview from consulting old destinations and avoids a cycle in which
rendering waits for the refresh it triggered.

## Diagrams run as a constrained local browser feature

A plain Mermaid fence becomes escaped source in a dedicated preview container. A
locally bundled browser program later turns that source into SVG. No renderer is
fetched from the network, and the document cannot select a different runtime.

The browser client disables diagram directives and frontmatter, uses strict rendering,
sets text and edge budgets, and does not install interactive callbacks. Rendering is
serialized because diagram initialization is shared mutable browser state. If source
or theme changes while rendering is asynchronous, the result is installed only when
the original container, markup, and theme are still current.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> SourceVisible
    SourceVisible --> Queued: diagram or theme observed
    Queued --> Rendering: serial queue begins attempt
    Rendering --> DiagramVisible: source container and theme still current
    Rendering --> ErrorVisible: current attempt fails safely
    Rendering --> Discarded: container, source, or theme changed
    DiagramVisible --> Queued: source or theme changes
    ErrorVisible --> Queued: source or theme changes
    Discarded --> [*]
```

The states describe one preview container, not the whole document. A discarded
result is not an error shown to the author; it is obsolete work. Failures that are
still current are rendered as text with an alert role and the original source, never
as untrusted HTML.

## Preview is useful precisely because it is not publication

The live view favors immediate, read-only feedback from visible buffers. Generated
HTML favors deterministic destinations and portable files. Print publication adds a
renderer-neutral document bridge, external tools, resource validation, and an
explicit command. Sharing semantic identities does not require collapsing these
hosts into one pipeline.

This distinction also explains the limits of preview. Its project membership begins
with the repository's last discovery, so a newly created path becomes available
through the editor's normal document and filesystem events rather than synchronous
rendering. Cross-document links may temporarily remain text while capabilities are
resolved. Invalid contributions may be shown as code without semantic decoration.
These are honest intermediate states, not partial generation.

The preview now gives each accepted reference a navigable identity. The next chapter
asks what happens when a reader wants more than one jump: an explorable view of the
fragment graph that remains useful in the presence of repetition, cycles, and large
projects.
